<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amdo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amdo is the north-eastern region of the Tibetan plateau, now mostly in the Chinese province of Qinghai with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. A frontier of grasslands and farming valleys where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, Chinese and Muslims met, it was home to the 11th-century kingdom of Tsongkha. It is the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a>, and holds great monasteries such as Kumbum and Labrang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amdo.jpg" alt="Amdo" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibetischer_Kulturraum_Karte.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
